    "innovation": "This correspondence presents a scientific debate on the likelihood and economic impact of a sudden Arctic methane release, while also providing historical context on the long-standing practice and risks of self-administered transcranial electrical stimulation.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "methane release",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Gail Whiteman and colleagues suggest that the opening up of the Arctic Ocean could bring more economic costs than benefits, owing to climatic effects resulting from a sudden release of 50 gigatonnes of methane from the area (Nature 499, 401-403; 2013).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "sea-ice decline",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "However, our literature review of the impact of sea-ice decline on Arctic greenhouse-gas exchange indicates that methane release is likely to be more gradual because of a slow rate of heat penetration into the subsea permafrost (see F. J. W. Parmentier et al. Nature Clim. Change 3, 195-202; 2013).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "methane release",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "East Siberian Arctic Shelf",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "We disagree with Gail Whiteman and colleagues that there is \"likely\" to be a large and sudden release of methane from the East Siberian Arctic Shelf (Nature 499, 401-403; 2013).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "sea-ice retreat",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Since 2005, accelerating sea-ice retreat in the Arctic has exposed the sea bed to much warmer conditions than those considered in earlier studies that anticipated a slow release of methane.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Arctic warming",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "We support the call for more rigorous integrated modelling of the costs and benefits of Arctic warming (G. Whiteman et al. Nature 499, 401-403; 2013).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Melting permafrost",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "high northern latitudes",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Melting permafrost, as Whiteman et al. show, gives rise to costly methane emissions. In addition, it damages the infrastructure needed for shipping and transportation, and for gas, oil and mineral mining, at high northern latitudes; this is because previously solid ground loses its structural integrity.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Melting permafrost",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arctic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The Madrid-based humanitarian organization DARA expects the cost of this damage in the Arctic to reach US$80 billion annually by 2030 (see go.nature.com/vnlzax).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Melting permafrost",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Russia",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "These losses will be unevenly distributed, with more than 90% likely to occur in Russia.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        }
